3.3.m:
Welcoming him with affection, the sage spoke with a smile:
Gautama said: / "Is all well with you, O king? Is your imperishable position safe?
Are your subjects prosperous, or the women of your inner quarters? / For what purpose have you come here, having cast aside all royal splendour?
And what are you pondering, O king, sighing long and hot?"
The king said: / "All of us are well, O brahmin, through your compassion. / For the fortunes of kings of noble lineage are indeed dependent on brahmins. / Yet what torments me is this piśācī of dreadful form,
unseen by anyone other than me, threatening me at every step.
Alas, what was done by me, burnt by a curse, is difficult to overcome;
no peace is produced from it by thousands of expiations.
Various sacrifices have been offered, with the entire treasury given as sacrificial fees;
the rivers and lakes that are worthy of veneration on the face of the earth have been bathed in;
all the sacred fields have been visited by me as I wandered;
all mantras have been recited; all deities have been meditated upon;
